Abstract—This work proposes a framework to train and deploy neural network-based gesture recognition algorithms in wearable devices. The approach is demonstrated for a highdensity electromyography (HD-EMG) gesture recognition system, where a Siamese convolution neural network (SCNN) learns to associate and dissociate muscle activity patterns from the same or distinct gesture classes. This optimizes learning in low-data environments such as gesture recognition and myoelectric control, where training data must be provided by the end user. Then, using a cosine similarity-based few-shot classifier and inter-sessionintra-user transfer of the SCNN’s learning, the proposed model is intended to achieve state-of-the-art results in a framework that is realistically applicable in wearable devices. For an experimented myoelectric interface user, the proposed model achieved 86.49 % accuracy in 6-class gesture recognition, using inter-session-intrauser transfer learning by the SCNN. Few-shot learning demonstrates convergence to this accuracy within 20 shots with the cosine similarity classifier. Across a group of 13 able-bodied participants, 90.66 % and 97.34 % mean and median accuracy were obtained from the 6-way 20-shot learning approach with a majority vote (100 votes) over time-distributed inferences.
Keywords—myoelectric control, electromyography, hand gesture, recognition, deep learning, body-machine interface, signal processing
I. INTRODUCTION
Artificial intelligence (AI) has propelled many fields of research in the last decade. Traditionally, the machine learning algorithms at the core of AI-based systems have relied on substantial datasets and high-performance computing to achieve ground-breaking results. This is especially true of modern deep neural network architectures with millions of trainable parameters that need to be stored and processed efficiently during training and inference. While tempting, the state-of-theart neural network architectures are not always compatible with applications such as wearable devices or other edge computing use cases with hardware-constrained resources and often few training data, notwithstanding real-time operation. In this paper, we tackle this issue for wearable gesture recognition systems where a fully embedded deployment is desirable for applications such as myoelectric prostheses. These devices need to operate with minimal latency in a real-time response, and they often require periodic calibration to maintain functionality and adapt to contextual changes. While deep learning-based classification of electromyography (EMG) signals has been successfully demonstrated in the literature [1]
[6], the conducted studies generally forgo deployability in embedded systems and other applicability concerns in the realworld, such as data availability. The typical supervised learning processes are designed to learn from large training datasets and are thus not optimal in the limited data environments of myoelectric control, where the end users need to contribute at least part of the training data. Few-shot learning is a machine learning paradigm that addresses learning with small datasets, by turning the learning process into a similarity comparison with one or more reference prototypes and aggregating the know-how of tasks that address the same abstract meta-problem [7], [8]. Over the past few years, it has been investigated from various perspectives, with the goal to learn from less and more general data [9]. This work’s motivation is two-fold, as there is the need in our target application for both streamlined embedded integration and optimization for small amounts of data. Addressing the latter, Rahimian et al. [10] propose an EMG time-series fewshot learning framework to optimize deep neural network training for small amounts of data. The approach uses temporal convolutions and attention mechanisms trained on sequences of labelled examples to infer on following unlabelled data. The study reports a promising 83.99 % 5-shot learning classification accuracy on a 5-class problem with data from the benchmark NinaPro dataset [11]. However, it does not consider real-time and embedded deployment. In prior work, we have demonstrated real-time gesture recognition from high-density electromyography (HD-EMG) signals using a standard convolutional neural network (CNN) architecture [2]. While transfer learning has been used to facilitate the calibration
