esulting system is an upper limb electromyography control interface that, once trained, can be deployed and calibrated using only feedforward flows in the neural network and few-shot vector accumulation and averaging.
II. METHOD
A. Sensor and HD-EMG Data
This work builds on wearable gesture recognition systems using HD-EMG to extract the spatial mapping of muscle activity
in the forearm, as displayed on Fig. 1 [14]-[16]. At a given sampling period, the individual samples of each channel of a 32electrode array are mapped to a corresponding 4 by 8 numerical array to produce an image of the spatial distribution of the motor unit action potentials (MUAP). An HD-EMG data point thus consists of a 2D array representing a muscle activity heatmap at a given moment as pictured on Fig. 1c. Hand gestures then generate a variety of muscle activity patterns from which spatial features can be extracted and learned by a neural network [2]. This approach is intended to be more robust to electrode position shifting than traditional lower electrode count and timefrequency domain processing methods [17]. Before the 2D sample mapping, the signal from each channel is filtered and preprocessed as described in [15] by analog band-pass filtering from 20 to 500 Hz, digital rectification and envelope detection/extraction with a moving average on 15 samples.
B. Siamese Convolutional Neural Network
The Siamese neural network has parallel inputs that go through three convolution layers with shared parameters as illustrated in Fig. 2. These layers extract features from the 2dimensional arrays representing the HD-EMG muscle activity maps. Each convolution block transforms its input data into a feature vector, often called embedding. The same convolution layer structure is used as in [14], with the 32 3x3 convolution kernels looking at finer details than the final 32 5x5 filters. Each convolution layer applies zero-padding to maintain input size throughout the network and is followed by a batch normalization layer and a leaky ReLU activation function with a negative slope of 0.01. The output is flattened to produce the feature vector embedding. In the siamese architecture, two input samples are fed in the twin blocks and their respective embedding can then be compared with a difference/similarity cost function. The proposed approach uses a triplet loss function [12] that takes three embeddings. An anchor embedding is produced from a given example, a positive embedding is produced from a distinct but similar example, and a negative embedding is produced from a dissimilar example. Analogous to the mean squared error (MSE), the proposed framework computes the triplet loss using a mean squared difference as a distance between 2 given embedding vectors a and b:
