C. Cosine Similarity Classifier
The classifier employed in the proposed model is based on cosine similarity. First, the siamese network is used to produce the feature embeddings of a given input data and representative data from each class. Then, the inferred class is the one deemed the closest to the input data by the cosine similarity function as displayed in the simplified 2-dimensional plane in Fig. 3. While this would conceptually call for a multi-channel Siamese network or an ensemble of 2-channel networks as pictured by Fig 2., it can be implemented efficiently by pre-computing classrepresentative embeddings from the training dataset. For each class, a mean embedding or prototype is produced from a training dataset by averaging the resulting vectors of the convolution layers block. At inference time, a single input data X is fedforward through the network, embedded into Xemb, and then compared with the pre-computed mean embeddings via the cosine similarity. This can be implemented mathematically as a dot product for a 1-to-1 comparison. For classification purposes, the mean class embeddings are transformed to unit vectors and put together in a matrix M. The product of M with the unit vector of Xemb yields a vector of distances where an argmax function is applied to infer a class belonging C as in:
