Since the cosine classifier’s training only consists of defining the mean embeddings by data accumulation and
averaging, it can be calibrated subsequently in the same manner. In practice, a given user initially trains the Siamese neural network and is then able to train and subsequently calibrate the classifier promptly and without resorting to resource intensive backpropagation. While the initial training session may need to be executed on a server or computer, the feedforward network can then be implemented on a more resource-efficient embedded platform that implements the classifier training and calibration.
D. Experiments and Datasets
The employed HD-EMG dataset was collected among 13 able-bodied male and female participants, most of which during a prior study [2]. New data was gathered using the same protocol. Participants were aged from 24 to 44 years old with no known medical condition, with a mix of both left and righthanded individuals. All measurements were performed on the right-hand limb. Informed consent was obtained from all participants and protocols were approved by the Comité plurifacultaire d’éthique de la recherche de l’Université Laval under approbation number 2019-268 phase 1 R-2 / 14-10-2021. One participant was familiar with EMG control interfaces and the sensor used, while the 12 others had little to no experience with such system. The dataset consists of forearm HD-EMG data collected while participants were holding the 6 hand gestures depicted in Fig. 4. A first dataset was collected from each participant where they would hold each gesture for 5 seconds one after the other with 5 seconds rest time in-between. This was then repeated 5 times with a 2-minute break between the 3rd and 4th repetitions. A second dataset was then collected during a different session to provide inter-session data for each user. This dataset is a shortened version of the first one where each gesture is held for 2 seconds and only repeated 3 times. The second dataset was recorded from 1 day to 6 months apart depending on the participant. To train the SCNN, we used a 5-fold cross-validation on the first dataset, splitting the shuffled set at 80% for training and 20% for validation on each iteration. Within the training subset, 8,500 triplets were formed for each class (1 reference data point, 1 other from the same class, and 1 randomly picked from a different class). For validation, 2,000 triplets per class were formed from the respective split. There is no test split yet at this stage since testing will be performed with the second dataset when the cosine classifier is appended to the SCNN. We used the ADAM optimizer with a learning rate of 0.01, a batch size of 64, and an alpha margin parameter of 0.2 for the triplet loss. Training was stopped after 5 epochs where the validation loss did not improve for more than 0.001, after which the best weights were restored. To evaluate the model’s ability to provide effective intersession, intra-user transfer learning capabilities, the cosine similarity classifier was evaluated with data from the second
