III. RESULTS
A. Siamese Convolutional Neural Network
Table I displays the number of epochs trained, triplet loss, and accuracy for each user. The triplet loss accuracy consists of evaluating whether the SCNN effectively produces a closer embedding for the same class data point using cosine similarity. With a mean and median triplet accuracy of 88.72 % and 93.35 % across all users on the validation set, the Siamese model demonstrates its ability to effectively discern whether two inputs are issued from the same gesture or not.
B. Cosine Similarity Classification Accuracy
Table II summarizes the accuracy of the cosine similarity classifier trained on new session data for all participants. The results show that the full set training accuracy (all-shot) can be reached within a 20-shot training process. Fig. 5 displays the convergence of n-shot accuracy for the 0-20 range where most users reach a plateau between 5 and 10 shots. For an experimented user, results are highly conclusive as the singlevote 86.49 % (all-shot) and 85.96 % (20-shot) are in-line with previous transfer learning convolutional neural network results of 84.03 %, which employed a more conventional architecture and framework that is not as deployment-friendly [2]. While single-vote results across unexperimented users are not as promising, leveraging time-distributed inferences with majority voting yields actionable results for real-time myoelectric control. As in [2], majority voting can be employed in real-time gesture recognition to smooth out the results and improve reliability. With HD-EMG data being sampled every 1 ms and the model inferring on each sample, there is headroom for postprocessing relative to the optimal controller delay of 100-150 ms [18]. Thus, we evaluated the majority vote accuracy for 1 to 100 votes using the 6-way 20-shot classifier. A median majority-vote accuracy of 97.34 % demonstrates near perfect performance for at least half of participants, while the mean is dragged slightly lower due to two outliers as observed in Fig. 6. In this figure,
convergence to 90-100% accuracy within 0 to 20 votes for most users can be observed.
IV. DISCUSSION AND CONCLUSION
The gesture recognition results of the proposed method are highly conclusive for an experimented user, and for naive users with the aid of majority vote inference to achieve state-of-theart results nearing 100% [19]. This demonstration is however limited to offline classification, which does not necessarily translate to equal real-time performance [20]. Thus, we interpret the results of this study as a proof of concept for the Siamese neural network and few-shot learning framework that enables further research and real-time integration of the approach. In future work, we aim to deploy and evaluate the proposed method with real-time performance metrics and real-world task completion exercises. We also aim to improve the individual performance of the SCNN and cosine similarity framework without majority voting across all users, which will consequently improve the downstream post-processing and system output performance and reliability. This contribution proposes a HD-EMG-based Siamese convolutional neural network and cosine similarity few-shot learning framework for embedded gesture recognition. With improvements in low-data performance and simplified efficient training/calibration, we aim to make deep learning-based solutions realistically applicable in wearable systems.
